(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-pharm-e3-pulmonary-vignettes"]={"title":"Pulmonary Infections, Asthma and Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease — Clinical Vignettes 1","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with diabetes took a course of amoxicillin six weeks ago and now has community-acquired pneumonia that will be treated at home with a beta-lactam plus a macrolide. Which beta-lactam fits this outpatient regimen?","choices":["Ceftriaxone","Cefepime","Imipenem/cilastatin","Cefpodoxime"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Age over 65, a beta-lactam within the past 3 months and diabetes are risk factors for penicillin-resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae; outpatients with them get a beta-lactam (amoxicillin with or without clavulanic acid, cefuroxime or cefpodoxime) plus a macrolide. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman with allergic asthma is prescribed inhaled cromolyn sodium ahead of a long stay in a home with cats. How should she take it?","choices":["At the first sign of wheezing","After her symptoms have started","In place of albuterol for attacks","Regularly, starting before exposure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mast cell stabilizers (cromolyn, nedocromil) must be taken before the antigen insult and taken regularly; they may take up to 4 weeks to work. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman with asthma develops wheezing every time she takes aspirin. Which oral controller is effective in aspirin-induced asthma?","choices":["Cromolyn sodium","Montelukast","Theophylline","Omalizumab"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Montelukast is an oral leukotriene D4 antagonist at the cysteinyl leukotriene 1 receptor (which mediates inflammation, bronchial constriction and mucus secretion) and is effective in aspirin-induced asthma. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man with a history of depression asks about starting varenicline to quit smoking. Which warning should he be counseled about?","choices":["Seizures","Neuropsychiatric events","Tachyarrhythmias","Hypertension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Varenicline, a partial nicotine receptor agonist, is listed with a black box warning for neuropsychiatric events (depression, suicidal ideation); its other side effects are nausea, sleep disturbance and headache. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is breathless only when hurrying or walking up a slight hill and had one exacerbation last year without hospitalization. Which drug treatment is suggested for him?","choices":["A short-acting bronchodilator as needed","A regular long-acting muscarinic antagonist","A regular long-acting beta-2 agonist","An inhaled steroid with a long-acting beta-2 agonist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mild or infrequent symptoms with 0 or 1 exacerbation a year and no hospitalization is group A, treated with a short-acting bronchodilator (a beta-2 agonist, a muscarinic antagonist or both) as needed. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman's cough from acute bronchitis has lasted more than five days, and testing documents Mycoplasma pneumoniae. Which antibiotic is preferred for her?","choices":["Doxycycline","Azithromycin","Levofloxacin","Cefuroxime"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When symptoms persist beyond 5 days, bacterial involvement is considered, and doxycycline is preferred if Mycoplasma is documented because of macrolide resistance. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman sets a quit date three weeks from now and is prescribed varenicline. When should she start taking it?","choices":["On the morning of her quit date","After her first smoke-free week","One week before her quit date","When cravings appear after quitting"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Varenicline, a partial nicotine receptor agonist, is started one week before the quit date and continued for 12 weeks. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease cannot generate a strong, deep inhalation. Which device should be avoided for him?","choices":["Dry powder inhaler","Nebulizer","Metered dose inhaler with a spacer","Inhaler with a valve holding chamber"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A dry powder inhaler relies on the patient's own inspiratory force to draw the powder out of the device, so it is not good for patients with poor inspiratory capacity. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman starting four-drug therapy for active pulmonary tuberculosis takes an oral contraceptive. Which drug in her regimen can make the contraceptive less reliable?","choices":["Isoniazid","Rifampin","Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rifampin induces cytochrome P450 enzymes, and contraceptives and anticoagulants are among the drugs it affects. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man taking isoniazid for tuberculosis develops numbness and tingling in his feet. Which vitamin should be given?","choices":["Thiamine (vitamin B1)","Folic acid (vitamin B9)","Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","Cobalamin (vitamin B12)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Isoniazid causes peripheral neuritis through pyridoxine deficiency, so pyridoxine (vitamin B6) is given. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with asthma cannot time pressing her metered dose inhaler with breathing in, but she wants to keep using it. Which addition helps her?","choices":["Pressing two puffs back to back","Breathing out into the mouthpiece","Skipping the breath-hold afterward","A spacer to hold the puff"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A metered dose inhaler requires the most coordination of the devices, and spacers are used to hold the medication for patients with poor coordination. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease uses tiotropium, inhaled fluticasone, albuterol as needed and theophylline. He develops nausea, vomiting and palpitations, then has a seizure. Which drug most likely caused this?","choices":["Tiotropium","Fluticasone","Theophylline","Albuterol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Theophylline has a narrow therapeutic index; nausea, vomiting and cardiac effects appear first, then seizures and arrhythmias as levels climb, which is why its plasma levels are monitored. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man receiving mechanical ventilation develops pneumonia on hospital day 8. He is started on cefepime plus tobramycin. Which drug must be added?","choices":["Azithromycin","Ciprofloxacin","Ertapenem","Vancomycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Late onset (5 days or more) calls for broad-spectrum therapy for multidrug-resistant pathogens: gram-negative combination therapy (cefepime plus an aminoglycoside here) plus gram-positive coverage with vancomycin or linezolid, since methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus and Pseudomonas must both be covered. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man who received intravenous antibiotics last month develops ventilator-associated pneumonia on hospital day 6. Which empiric regimen is appropriate?","choices":["Meropenem, piperacillin-tazobactam and vancomycin","Piperacillin-tazobactam, tobramycin and linezolid","Vancomycin, linezolid and levofloxacin","Ceftriaxone, azithromycin and vancomycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Risk factors for multidrug-resistant pathogens call for broad coverage: an antipseudomonal beta-lactam (piperacillin-tazobactam) plus an aminoglycoside (tobramycin) plus gram-positive coverage (linezolid). — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with severe allergic asthma receives his first subcutaneous omalizumab injection. Which serious adverse effect is listed for this drug?","choices":["Oral thrush","Seizures","Anaphylaxis","Tremor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Omalizumab's listed adverse effects include anaphylaxis (about 0.1%), urticaria, gastrointestinal disturbances, injection site pain, bruising, neutralizing antibodies and secondary malignancies. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman six weeks into four-drug tuberculosis therapy says traffic-light colors look washed out and she struggles to tell red from green. Which drug is responsible?","choices":["Isoniazid","Rifampin","Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ethambutol's adverse effects include visual disturbances: decreased red-green discrimination and decreased visual acuity. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with worsening kidney function has late-onset hospital-acquired pneumonia and needs coverage for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. Which drug needs no dose adjustment for her kidney function?","choices":["Vancomycin","Linezolid","Tobramycin","Cefepime"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Linezolid gives great methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus coverage with good lung penetration, comes in intravenous and oral forms, and needs no dose adjustment based on renal function. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman stung by a bee develops hives, wheezing, throat tightness and low blood pressure. She is conscious and has a pulse. Which treatment acts on both her blood pressure and her airways?","choices":["Intravenous epinephrine","Intravenous diphenhydramine","Inhaled albuterol","Intramuscular epinephrine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine acts on alpha receptors (vasoconstriction, decreased vascular permeability) and beta receptors (bronchial smooth muscle relaxation), and intramuscular injection is preferred unless the patient is coding. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has had a cough, low-grade fever and chest soreness for three days, starting after a cold. He is diagnosed with acute bronchitis and is otherwise well. Which approach is recommended?","choices":["Symptomatic care, no antibiotic","A macrolide such as azithromycin","Doxycycline for possible Mycoplasma","A respiratory fluoroquinolone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute bronchitis is usually viral (rhinovirus, coronavirus, influenza, adenovirus) and generally self-limited, so care is symptomatic and supportive (analgesics, antipyretics, antitussives, fluids); routine antibiotics are not recommended. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man develops anaphylaxis minutes after receiving an antibiotic, becomes pulseless, and cardiopulmonary resuscitation begins. By which route should his epinephrine now be given?","choices":["Intramuscular","Intravenous","Subcutaneous","Inhaled"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine can be given intramuscularly, subcutaneously or intravenously; intramuscular is generally preferred unless the patient is coding, as he now is. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man with active pulmonary tuberculosis asks why he must take several drugs instead of one. What is the main reason?","choices":["To prevent drug resistance","Because the bacteria divide rapidly","To lower the risk of liver damage","To allow a lower dose of each drug"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Single agents are not used in active disease, to prevent the development of resistance; rifampin is rarely used alone because of rapid tolerance, and ethambutol resistance develops quickly if it is used alone. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 36"}]};
